import sys
p = sys.argv[1]
lines = open(p, encoding='utf-8').read().split('\n')
idx = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith('| 582 |')]
assert len(idx) == 1
lines.insert(idx[0] + 1, "| 583 | 2026-10-20 | **Scales lessons 10 and 11 (her order, 13:07).** 10 `sc-chrom` The chromatic scale (moved in from Tones; her `CHROM_UP`, `CHROM_DOWN`, `chromFinger`): every key, one half step at a time, twelve steps from C to C (twelve S pills); going up, black keys written with sharps; coming down, with flats; right-hand and left-hand fingering (3 on every black key, thumb on the white keys, 2 on F and C for the right hand, on E and B for the left) shown in two staggered rows with a ring round every thumb so the digits do not clump; her turn: pick a start note (C D E F G A) and tap the twelve next keys, a wrong key says so and points to the very next key. 11 `sc-min` Three kinds of minor on A (her `MinorFormsCard` texts, `MINOR_FORMS`, `STEP_PATTERNS`): the three step rows together, then natural, harmonic (7th raised, T+S) and melodic (6th and 7th raised going up; coming down = natural), changed notes in orange; her turn: choose a form and tap its eight notes, with a wrong-key message that names the step to take (S, T or T+S). New `ScPillRow` (any number of step pills incl. T+S); `ScKeys` gets `ring` (rings on thumbs in the staggered-names mode). Home p-scale lessons now 1-11; old chromatic / minor-forms tools untouched. Next: rebuild the Scales drill chips to match lessons 1-11. Korean wording is mine, unverified. Built by `patch589.py` + the `ring` edit (`t592`). Checked in English and Korean, all rounds complete, no errors. Local commit only, not pushed. |")
open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write('\n'.join(lines)); print('ok')
